sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-30> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because the proof processes of almost every area of mathematics are computationally irreducible, adding axioms cannot remove the problem: there will essentially always be theorems that are short to state but whose shortest proofs are very long.";
    dc:type "mathematical";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Computational irreducibility makes short theorems with only long proofs inevitable";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Implications for Mathematics and Its Foundations” (from p. 772), p. 779. Scope: axiom systems of mathematics, idealized as multiway systems"
}
